;; delimited-terminator's three records, in a file whose last record carries no
;; delimiter at all.
;;
;; `optional-terminator` is the placement that admits both days of the shop's
;; extract: a delimiter follows every record, except that the file may end after
;; the last record without one. A reader is not lenient about it — it is what
;; the file node says the file does — and a writer is not lenient about it
;; either: under this placement a writer MUST emit the final delimiter rather
;; than choosing whether to, because two writers left to decide produce two
;; files from one descriptor.
;;
;; That is why this entry's input.bin is twenty-three bytes and the file its
;; records are written back into is twenty-four. The two are not the same bytes
;; and they decode to the same records, which is the property the corpus checks
;; the writing direction by.
(encoding
  (charset cp037)
  (sign-convention ebcdic)
  (byte-order big-endian)
  (float-format ieee-754))

(framing
  (recfm line-sequential)
  (delimiter (bytes "15"))
  (placement optional-terminator))

(record LINE
  (copybook "line.cpy" LINE-RECORD))

(discriminate LINE single-record-type)

(sequence (* LINE))
